course: books
lesson: 18
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter IX, part 2: The Mock Turtle's Story (school under the sea)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Queen wakes a Gryphon sleeping in the sun (a creature that is half eagle, half lion), orders it to take Alice to the Mock Turtle, and goes off to see about «some executions». The Gryphon chuckles: it's all her fancy, «they never executes nobody». So the beheadings are only shouting.
explain: They find the Mock Turtle sitting sad and lonely on a ledge of rock, sighing as if his heart would break. «It's all his fancy,» says the Gryphon again: «he hasn't got no sorrow». The Mock Turtle tells them to sit and not speak until he has finished — and then says nothing for ages, until at last: «Once I was a real Turtle.»
explain: He tells about his school in the sea. The master was an old Turtle they called Tortoise, «because he taught us». Whenever Alice asks a sensible question, the two creatures call her dull and tell her to be ashamed, and Alice feels ready to sink into the earth.
explain: The school subjects are all school words gone wrong: Reeling and Writhing (reading and writing), Ambition, Distraction, Uglification and Derision (addition, subtraction, multiplication and division), Mystery and Seaography, Drawling, Stretching and Fainting in Coils, and Laughing and Grief.
explain: Lessons lasted ten hours the first day, nine the next, and so on: «That's the reason they're called lessons, because they lessen from day to day.» Alice works out that the eleventh day must have been a holiday, but when she asks about the twelfth, the Gryphon quickly changes the subject.
fact: A Gryphon (also spelled griffin) is a creature from old stories, half eagle and half lion; the book says «If you don't know what a Gryphon is, look at the picture».
fact: The Gryphon says of the Queen: «It's all her fancy, that: they never executes nobody, you know.» Its grammar is country talk; in standard English: they never execute anybody.
fact: «Everybody says "come on!" here,» thinks Alice: «I never was so ordered about in all my life, never!»
fact: The Mock Turtle sits «sad and lonely on a little ledge of rock»; the Gryphon says «he hasn't got no sorrow» — it's all his fancy (imagination).
fact: «This here young lady, she wants for to know your history, she do» is the Gryphon's country way of saying: this young lady wants to know your story.
fact: Alice thinks: «I don't see how he can ever finish, if he doesn't begin.»
fact: «Once I was a real Turtle» — the Mock Turtle is sad because he is only a «mock» (pretend) turtle; his story is broken now and then by the Gryphon's odd cry «Hjckrrh!» and his own heavy sobbing.
fact: «We went to school in the sea, though you mayn't believe it—» «I never said I didn't!» says Alice; «You did,» says the Mock Turtle, though she had said nothing of the kind.
fact: When he boasts «we went to school every day», Alice says «I've been to a day-school, too; you needn't be so proud as all that.»
fact: The old Turtle master was called Tortoise «because he taught us»: say «taught us» quickly and it sounds like «tortoise».
fact: Extras at the sea school: «French, music, and washing»; Alice's school taught French and music but not washing, and she says «You couldn't have wanted it much, living at the bottom of the sea.» «Extras» were lessons parents paid more for.
fact: The regular course: Reeling and Writhing (reading and writing); Ambition, Distraction, Uglification and Derision (addition, subtraction, multiplication and division); the Gryphon asks if she knows what «beautify» is, and Alice says doubtfully «it means—to—make—anything—prettier», so «uglify» must mean the opposite.
fact: More subjects: Mystery, ancient and modern (history), with Seaography (geography); Drawling, Stretching and Fainting in Coils (drawing, sketching and painting in oils), taught by an old conger-eel once a week.
fact: The Classics master was an old crab who taught «Laughing and Grief» (Latin and Greek, the languages studied as «classics»); remembering him, «both creatures hid their faces in their paws».
fact: Lessons: «Ten hours the first day, nine the next, and so on»; «they're called lessons, because they lessen from day to day» (lessen = get smaller).
fact: Alice says «Then the eleventh day must have been a holiday?»; when she asks about the twelfth, the Gryphon says «That's enough about lessons».
fact: Alice's own sums went wrong in chapter II («four times five is twelve»), and now the sea-school's subjects go wrong too.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «skibidi» = a silly word kids shout, with no real meaning; «chur» (New Zealand) = thanks, cool; «fixin' to» (Southern USA) = about to; «grand» (Ireland) = fine, good.
exercise: Where do Alice and the Queen find the Gryphon? | Lying fast asleep in the sun | Painting roses in the garden | Swimming in the sea | Playing croquet with a flamingo
exercise: Why does the Queen leave Alice with the Gryphon? | She must go back and see after some executions she has ordered | She is tired and needs a nap in the shade after all the shouting | She is afraid of the Gryphon | She wants soup
exercise: «What fun!» says the Gryphon. What does it find funny? | The Queen: it's all her fancy, they never execute anybody | Alice's hair, which wants cutting very badly indeed | The Mock Turtle's song | The game of croquet
exercise: «Everybody says "come on!" here.» How does Alice feel about this? | She's never been so ordered about in all her life | Pleased, because everyone is so friendly and helpful to her | Sleepy | Proud
exercise: Where is the Mock Turtle sitting? | Sad and lonely on a little ledge of rock | Happily on the sandy beach, dancing with a crowd of lobsters | In a big soup tureen | At the bottom of a well
exercise: «What is his sorrow?» asks Alice. What does the Gryphon answer? | «It's all his fancy, that: he hasn't got no sorrow» | «He lost his shell at school a long, long time ago, poor thing» | «The Queen wants him for soup» | «He can't dance»
exercise: What does the Mock Turtle tell Alice and the Gryphon before he starts? | Sit down and don't speak a word till he has finished | Stand up straight and repeat a lesson to him, with hands folded | Bring him some soup | Look at the picture
exercise: «I don't see how he can ever finish, if he doesn't begin.» Why does Alice think this? | He says nothing at all for some minutes | He talks far too fast for anybody to follow him | He has already finished | He is singing
exercise: What are the first words of the Mock Turtle's story? | «Once I was a real Turtle» | «Once upon a time there were three little sisters» | «Fury said to a mouse» | «William the Conqueror»
exercise: What strange sound does the Gryphon make now and then? | «Hjckrrh!» | «Wow! wow! wow!» | «Sh! sh! sh!» | «Hush! Hush!»
exercise: Where did the Mock Turtle go to school? | In the sea | Under a mushroom | In the Queen's palace | In a treacle-well
exercise: Why did they call the old Turtle master «Tortoise»? | Because he taught us | Because he was really a tortoise in disguise and walked very slowly | Because he was very old | Because he lived on land
exercise: When Alice asks about «Tortoise», what does the Mock Turtle say to her? | «Really you are very dull!» | «What a clever question, my dear» | «I don't know» | «Ask the Gryphon»
exercise: «We had the best of educations — in fact, we went to school every day.» What does Alice say? | «I've been to a day-school, too; you needn't be so proud as all that» | «I've never been to a school at all, so I'm very jealous of you» | «How lovely» | «Every day? How tiring»
exercise: What «extras» did the sea school have? | French, music, and washing | Croquet, painting and soup | Latin, Greek and dancing | Riddles and treacle
exercise: What did the Mock Turtle learn instead of reading and writing? | Reeling and Writhing | Ambition and Distraction | Drawling and Stretching | Uglification and Derision
exercise: Which of the Mock Turtle's subjects stands for multiplication? | Uglification | Ambition | Derision | Distraction
exercise: The Gryphon asks Alice if she knows what «beautify» means. What does she say? | «It means — to — make — anything — prettier» | «It means to make anything uglier» | «It means to sing» | «It means to wash»
exercise: Who taught «Drawling, Stretching, and Fainting in Coils»? | An old conger-eel who came once a week | An old crab with one claw, who came every day | An old Turtle called Tortoise | The Gryphon
exercise: What did the Classics master teach, «they used to say»? | Laughing and Grief | Reeling and Writhing | Mystery and Seaography | French and washing
exercise: Who was the Classics master? | An old crab | An old conger-eel | The Duchess herself | The Dormouse
exercise: How many hours of lessons were there on the first day? | Ten | Nine | Twelve | One
exercise: Why, says the Gryphon, are they called lessons? | They lessen from day to day | They are less fun than games | They are taught down in the sea | They make you feel less tired
exercise: What does Alice work out about the eleventh day? | It must have been a holiday | It must have had eleven hours of lessons | It was the hardest day | It was exam day
exercise: What happens when Alice asks about the twelfth day? | The Gryphon says «That's enough about lessons» | The Mock Turtle explains it very carefully and slowly, with a sum | Alice answers herself | They all go to sleep
test: Noah: «the Gryphon reckons the Queen chops heads off all day, deffo.» (deffo = definitely) Put it right. | The Gryphon says «they never executes nobody» — it's all her fancy | Right: the Gryphon says she has already chopped off a hundred heads today, and counted them | The Gryphon says only the King does it | He says nothing about her
test: «They never executes nobody» — say it in standard English. | They never execute anybody | They always execute everybody | They execute nobody's heads but the King's | Nobody ever executes them
test: Why might the Mock Turtle be so sad, from what he says first? | He was once a real Turtle and now is only a «mock» one | He has lost the soup recipe that his grandmother gave him long ago | He failed his exams | The Queen beheaded his family
test: Ava from Texas: «I'm fixin' to say Tortoise was a real tortoise.» (fixin' to = about to) Put it right. | He was an old Turtle, called Tortoise as a pun on «taught us» | Right: he was a giant tortoise who walked up out of the sea every morning to teach | He was a crab | He was the Gryphon
test: «Yes, we went to school in the sea, though you mayn't believe it—» «I never said I didn't!» «You did.» Why is this unfair to Alice? | She had not said anything of the kind, but they insist she did | She really did say it, twice, and loudly, so they were right to be cross | The Gryphon said it | Nobody was talking
test: The Mock Turtle is relieved that Alice's school did not teach washing. What does «extra» mean here? | A lesson parents had to pay more for | A lesson that is extra long and goes on after dark | A lesson only for turtles | A lesson that is extra easy
test: «You couldn't have wanted it much, living at the bottom of the sea.» What is Alice's joke about washing? | You're always wet under the sea anyway, so why learn washing? | The sea is too dirty to wash in, so the lessons were all a waste | Turtles can't hold soap | Washing is only for clothes
test: Match the pun: «Mystery, ancient and modern, with Seaography». Which real school subjects are hidden here? | History and geography | Maths and music lessons | Reading and writing | French and Latin
test: «Fainting in Coils» hides which real lesson? | Painting in oils | Swimming in coils | Sketching in pencil | Dancing in circles
test: Jack, chur (thanks): «so why'd the Turtle and the Gryphon hide their faces in their paws?» What's the best answer? | They were sad remembering «Laughing and Grief» and their old master | They were playing hide and seek with Alice on the rocks by the sea | Alice had been rude | The sun was in their eyes
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Alice asks lots of questions and the creatures are always patient with her.» | False: they call her dull and tell her to be ashamed and to hold her tongue | True: they praise every question she asks and answer each one kindly | True, but only the Gryphon | False: Alice asks nothing
test: Looking back: at the tea-party, when Alice asked what happens when they «come to the beginning again», what happened? | The March Hare changed the subject — just as the Gryphon does about the twelfth day | The Hatter gave a clear answer with a long explanation about cups | Alice was sent home | The Dormouse explained it
test: Looking back: in chapter II, Alice tested herself with sums. What did she say? | «Four times five is twelve, and four times six is thirteen» | «Two and two is four, and four and four is eight, and eight and eight is sixteen» | «Ten, nine, eight, seven» | «Ambition, Distraction, Uglification»
test: Looking back: what was the first sentence in Alice's French lesson-book, which frightened the Mouse in chapter II? | «Où est ma chatte?» (Where is my cat?) | «Bonjour, Monsieur Souris» (Hello, Mr Mouse) | «J'aime le thé» | «Off with his head!»
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Mouse said a history lesson was «the driest thing I know». Why did he recite it? | To dry everyone off after the pool of tears | To teach Alice all about the Queen of Hearts and her family | To put the Lory to sleep | To win a prize
test: Looking back: in chapter I, Alice used «Latitude» and «Longitude». Why? | She had no idea what they meant, but thought they were nice grand words | She was a map-maker | Her sister asked her | The White Rabbit told her
test: ★★★ Lessons were ten hours the first day, nine the next, and so on. How many hours in all over the first ten days? | 55 | 100 | 45 | 10
test: ★★★ If the lessons lessen by one hour a day, why is the twelfth day a puzzle? | It would need minus one hour of lessons | It would need twelve hours of lessons, as many as the day's number | It would be a Sunday | The school would close
test: ★★★ Ambition, Distraction, Uglification, Derision. Which real word does «Distraction» hide, and what letters change? | Subtraction: «Sub» becomes «Dis» | Addition: «Add» becomes «Dis» | Division: «Div» becomes «Dis» | Multiplication: «Multi» becomes «Dis»
exam: Identify the reason assigned by the Mock Turtle for the designation «Tortoise» as applied to his former master. | That he taught them | That he was a tortoise by birth | That he moved with great slowness | That he lived upon dry land
exam: It is stated that the duration of lessons diminished by one hour per day commencing at ten. Ascertain the condition of the eleventh day. | It was a holiday | It comprised eleven hours of instruction | It was devoted to washing | It was the day of examination
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the Gryphon's opinion of the Queen's sentences of execution. | That they were her fancy and that nobody was in fact executed | That they were carried out promptly and in full by the soldiers of the Queen | That they applied only to turtles | That they were just
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the regular course of the Mock Turtle's education? | It comprised Reeling and Writhing and the four branches of Arithmetic | It comprised French, music and washing only, these being included without extra charge | It was taught by the Gryphon | It lasted twelve hours each day
